blocks.body:
For a flow $f$ in a network, the following are equivalent:

1. $f$ is a maximum flow;
2. there is no augmenting path for $f$;
3. $|f| = c(S,T)$ for some $s$–$t$ cut $(S,T)$.

Consequently, whenever a maximum flow exists (in particular for every finite network), the maximum flow value equals the minimum cut capacity.

The running time of plain Ford–Fulkerson can depend on the size of the capacities, and with irrational capacities a bad choice of augmenting paths may never terminate. Edmonds and Karp showed that always choosing a shortest augmenting path (found by breadth-first search) needs at most $O(|V||E|)$ augmentations, so $O(|V||E|^2)$ time in total, whatever the capacities. Much faster algorithms exist; in 2022 Chen, Kyng, Liu, Peng, Probst Gutenberg and Sachdeva gave an algorithm that, for integer capacities of polynomial size, runs in almost-linear time $|E|^{1+o(1)}$.

Many problems become flow problems after a small change to the network:

- several sources or sinks: add a supersource joined to every source (and a supersink) by edges of unlimited capacity;
- a capacity on a vertex $v$: split $v$ into $v_{\text{in}} \to v_{\text{out}}$ with that capacity on the new edge;
- an undirected edge of capacity $c$: replace it by two opposite directed edges of capacity $c$.

Integral flows also decompose into paths: an integer flow of value $k$ can be split into $k$ unit flows along $s$–$t$ paths, plus possibly some cycles. With all capacities 1, this gives Menger's theorem: the maximum number of edge-disjoint $s$–$t$ paths equals the minimum number of edges whose removal destroys every directed $s$–$t$ path.

